        "content": "## PROF1: Professional Behavior — Level 4 / 5 · Proficient\nPediatric Hematology-Oncology milestone · levels 1–5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 4 · Proficient — the highest level fully demonstrated; the ceiling at 4 is set in part by the simulation (Level 5's coaching-of-others and community/profession engagement not assessable in a single family encounter), not solely by a learner shortfall.\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1–4 demonstrated; Level 5 not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity).\n\n## Evidence by level\n### Level 1 · Novice — Demonstrated\nThe learner demonstrates knowledge of expected professional behavior and the triggers for lapses, opening a charged, accusatory encounter with a nondefensive, respectful acknowledgment rather than argument. The opening charge that nobody listens to Black patients was a clear potential trigger, and the learner named the frustration and stayed engaged.\nLearner said: \"I hear the frustration in your voice ... I'm really sorry\"\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner — Demonstrated\nThe learner demonstrates accountability for patient care, committing to directness and honesty and following through across the encounter.\nLearner said: \"I would love to be direct with you ... really hear about what's been going on and how we can be doing better\"\n\n### Level 3 · Competent — Demonstrated\nThe learner maintains professional behavior in an increasingly stressful and complex situation — a mistrustful parent, adherence concerns, and difficult questions about long-term safety. Most notably, the learner answers an uncomfortable evidence question with honesty rather than deflection, acknowledging the limits of the data.\nLearner said: \"I can't answer directly for you ... we don't have that data ... I'll have better answers for you\"\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient — Demonstrated\nThe learner exhibits a clear sense of duty to patient care and professional responsibility, recognizing the forces that could trigger a professionalism lapse (defensiveness in the face of accusation, the temptation to give a reassuring but untrue answer) and intervening to prevent them. Rather than dressing up the safety question, the learner names what is known and unknown, and the learner builds a collaborative, patient-centered plan that centers the adolescent's voice.\nLearner said: \"My hope really is to be as truthful as possible with you\"\nLearner said: \"make a plan that feels potentially feasible ... then we, um, propose it to Jordan and see how he feels about it\"\n\n### Level 5 · Expert — Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nLevel 5 turns on modeling professional behavior and coaching others whose behavior falls short, and on extending the role beyond individual patient care into community, specialty, and profession. A single family encounter with no colleagues or trainees present offers no occasion to coach another professional or to engage the broader community, so this level cannot be assessed here; it does not count against the learner.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner met every assessable behavior through Level 4. Facing an opening accusation about racism and medication harm, the learner stayed nondefensive, named the parent's frustration, and committed to directness. Through escalating pressure — adherence doubts, long-term safety questions, demands not to be given a pretty answer — the learner held composure and, critically, told the truth about the limits of the evidence rather than offering false reassurance. This is the hallmark of a sense of duty under pressure: the learner recognized the pull toward a reassuring lie and intervened against it, preserving trust. The learner then converted a hostile start into a shared, adolescent-centered plan, demonstrating duty to the patient's care beyond simply surviving the conversation.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 5 is defined by coaching others whose behavior fails professional expectations and by extending the pediatric hematologist/oncologist role into the community, specialty, and profession. Neither arose: no colleague or trainee was present to coach, and the encounter was confined to one family. The assessment holds at Level 4 because the simulation could not surface Level 5 behaviors (no opportunity), not because the learner demonstrably fell short of them.\n\n## Recommendations\nTo continue strengthening Level 4 and build toward Level 5, keep anticipating trigger points before they escalate and name them explicitly. One place the learner leaned on a weaker, authority-based reassurance before recovering was the first safety answer; a stronger, more consistently honest framing from the start would be, for example Suggested wording: \"Honestly, we have strong evidence it reduces crises, but because kids with sickle cell didn't used to live into their forties, we don't yet have decades of long-term safety data — I want to be straight with you about what we know and what we don't.\". In team settings, look for chances to model this nondefensive, truth-telling stance aloud so trainees and colleagues can learn from it — the coaching behavior that distinguishes Level 5.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — The learner defused an openly distrustful, racially charged opening without defensiveness, committed to honesty, and delivered it even when it was uncomfortable, acknowledging the genuine gaps in long-term safety data. The learner turned the encounter into a collaborative plan that centered the adolescent's voice and the family's lived constraints.\n- **Priority gaps** — One early safety answer relied on authority (it wouldn't be recommended if it weren't safe) before the learner recovered with a candid account of the evidence; leading with candor would be stronger. Level 5 behaviors — coaching others and engaging the wider profession — were not assessable in this single-family encounter.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Lead with honest acknowledgment of uncertainty from the first safety question rather than falling back on authority, then recovering.\n  2. Continue anticipating professionalism triggers (accusation, pressure for false reassurance) and naming them explicitly.\n  3. In team settings, model and voice this nondefensive, truth-telling stance so others can learn from it.\n",
